**Claim 1.** A method for characterizing rock permeability across a plurality of locations in a subsurface region, the method comprising:

**(a)** acquiring seismic data over the subsurface region, the subsurface region encompassing the plurality of locations;

**(b)** processing the acquired seismic data to derive a seismic velocity field representative of seismic wave propagation velocities at each of the plurality of locations within the subsurface region;

**(c)** obtaining a plurality of rock samples, each rock sample corresponding to a respective sampling location within the subsurface region;

**(d)** performing laboratory analysis on each of the plurality of rock samples to measure a set of petrophysical properties for each rock sample, the set of petrophysical properties including at least a measured permeability value and a measured seismic velocity value;

**(e)** establishing a correlation relationship between the measured seismic velocity values and the measured permeability values derived from the laboratory analysis of the plurality of rock samples;

**(f)** calibrating the correlation relationship against the derived seismic velocity field at the respective sampling locations to generate a calibrated velocity-permeability transform;

**(g)** applying the calibrated velocity-permeability transform to the derived seismic velocity field to estimate a permeability value at each of the plurality of locations within the subsurface region;

**(h)** generating a spatial permeability distribution model of the subsurface region from the estimated permeability values across the plurality of locations; and

**(i)** outputting the spatial permeability distribution model to characterize rock permeability across the plurality of locations in the subsurface region.

---

*Optional dependent claims you might append:*

**Claim 2.** The method of claim 1, wherein processing the acquired seismic data comprises performing seismic inversion to derive a compressional-wave velocity field, a shear-wave velocity field, or both.

**Claim 3.** The method of claim 1, wherein establishing the correlation relationship comprises applying a regression analysis or a machine-learning model trained on the measured petrophysical properties.

**Claim 4.** The method of claim 1, further comprising validating the spatial permeability distribution model against well-log-derived permeability data at one or more borehole locations within the subsurface region.

**Claim 5.** The method of claim 1, wherein the set of petrophysical properties further includes porosity, bulk density, or fluid saturation, and wherein the calibrated velocity-permeability transform is further conditioned on at least one of said properties.
